{"slug":"strobogrammatic-numbers","title":"Numbers That Look the Same Upside Down","topic":"backtracking","difficulty":"medium","order":444,"tags":["recursion","strings","enumeration"],"timeLimitMs":2000,"concept":{"title":"Build from the middle outwards in matching pairs","body":["Turn a number upside down (rotate it by 180 degrees): `0`, `1` and `8` look the same, `6` becomes `9` and `9` becomes `6`, other digits are not valid. A number is **strobogrammatic** if it reads the same after the rotation. The first digit and the last digit must be a pair `(a, b)` from `00`, `11`, `88`, `69`, `96`, and the digits between them form a shorter strobogrammatic number.",{"code":"def build(m, n):            # all strobogrammatic strings of length m; n is the full length\n    if m == 0:\n        return [\"\"]\n    if m == 1:\n        return [\"0\", \"1\", \"8\"]      # the middle digit is its own mirror\n    result = []\n    for mid in build(m - 2, n):\n        for a, b in ((\"0\", \"0\"), (\"1\", \"1\"), (\"6\", \"9\"), (\"8\", \"8\"), (\"9\", \"6\")):\n            if m == n and a == \"0\":\n                continue               # no leading zero in the whole number\n            result.append(a + mid + b)\n    return result"},"The recursion goes from `m` to `m - 2`, wrapping a pair around every shorter answer. The pair `00` is allowed inside but never as the outermost pair (the number would start with 0), except for the single digit `0`. The base cases are the empty string and the three one-digit numbers."]},"statement":["A number is *strobogrammatic* if it looks the same when rotated by 180 degrees (the digits `0`, `1`, `8` stay, `6` and `9` swap, no other digit can be used). Print all strobogrammatic numbers with exactly `n` digits in increasing order, one per line. A number does not start with 0, except the number 0 itself when `n = 1`."],"inputFormat":["The only line has `n`."],"outputFormat":["The numbers in increasing order, one per line."],"constraints":["1 <= n <= 9"],"examples":[{"input":"2\n","output":"11\n69\n88\n96\n","note":"The numbers are 11, 69, 88 and 96."}],"hints":["The outer pair of digits is one of 00, 11, 88, 69, 96; the inside is a shorter answer.","Do not allow 0 as the first digit of the whole number.","Length 0 gives one empty string, length 1 gives 0, 1 and 8."],"starter":{"python":"n = int(input())\n\n# Your code here\n","cpp":"#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    long long n;\n    cin >> n;\n\n    // Your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","java":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long n = sc.nextLong();\n\n        // Your code here\n    }\n}\n"},"solution":{"explanation":["The answers of length `m` are the answers of length `m - 2` wrapped in one of the pairs 00, 11, 69, 88, 96 (without 00 on the outside). Lengths 0 and 1 are the base cases. All strings of one length sort the same as the numbers."],"complexity":"Time O(count * n), where count is at most 1500.","code":{"python":"def build(m, n):\n    if m == 0:\n        return [\"\"]\n    if m == 1:\n        return [\"0\", \"1\", \"8\"]\n    result = []\n    for mid in build(m - 2, n):\n        for a, b in ((\"0\", \"0\"), (\"1\", \"1\"), (\"6\", \"9\"), (\"8\", \"8\"), (\"9\", \"6\")):\n            if m == n and a == \"0\":\n                continue\n            result.append(a + mid + b)\n    return result\n\nn = int(input())\n\nprint(\"\\n\".join(sorted(build(n, n))))\n","cpp":"#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n#include <algorithm>\nusing namespace std;\n\nvector<string> build(int m, int n) {\n    if (m == 0) return {\"\"};\n    if (m == 1) return {\"0\", \"1\", \"8\"};\n    vector<string> result;\n    const char* pairs[5][2] = {{\"0\", \"0\"}, {\"1\", \"1\"}, {\"6\", \"9\"}, {\"8\", \"8\"}, {\"9\", \"6\"}};\n    for (const string& mid : build(m - 2, n)) {\n        for (int p = 0; p < 5; p++) {\n            if (m == n && p == 0) continue;\n            result.push_back(string(pairs[p][0]) + mid + pairs[p][1]);\n        }\n    }\n    return result;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    long long n;\n    cin >> n;\n\n    vector<string> out = build((int)n, (int)n);\n    sort(out.begin(), out.end());\n    for (const string& s : out) cout << s << \"\\n\";\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","java":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static List<String> build(int m, int n) {\n        if (m == 0) return new ArrayList<>(List.of(\"\"));\n        if (m == 1) return new ArrayList<>(List.of(\"0\", \"1\", \"8\"));\n        String[][] pairs = {{\"0\", \"0\"}, {\"1\", \"1\"}, {\"6\", \"9\"}, {\"8\", \"8\"}, {\"9\", \"6\"}};\n        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (String mid : build(m - 2, n)) {\n            for (int p = 0; p < 5; p++) {\n                if (m == n && p == 0) continue;\n                result.add(pairs[p][0] + mid + pairs[p][1]);\n            }\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long n = sc.nextLong();\n\n        List<String> out = build((int) n, (int) n);\n        Collections.sort(out);\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (String s : out) sb.append(s).append(\"\\n\");\n        System.out.print(sb);\n    }\n}\n"}},"tests":[{"input":"1\n","output":"0\n1\n8\n"},{"input":"2\n","output":"11\n69\n88\n96\n"},{"input":"3\n","output":"101\n111\n181\n609\n619\n689\n808\n818\n888\n906\n916\n986\n"},{"input":"4\n","output":"1001\n1111\n1691\n1881\n1961\n6009\n6119\n6699\n6889\n6969\n8008\n8118\n8698\n8888\n8968\n9006\n9116\n9696\n9886\n9966\n"},{"input":"5\n","output":"10001\n10101\n10801\n11011\n11111\n11811\n16091\n16191\n16891\n18081\n18181\n18881\n19061\n19161\n19861\n60009\n60109\n60809\n61019\n61119\n61819\n66099\n66199\n66899\n68089\n68189\n68889\n69069\n69169\n